+# Stock reservations (holds) — spec
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout needs to hold stock for a cart while the customer pays, so the units
+cannot be sold to someone else in the meantime. After payment the hold is either
+turned into a normal order or let go. Carts are abandoned all the time, so a
+hold nobody confirms must lapse on its own; there is no background job, so the
+lapse has to take effect purely from the injected clock. The cart service
+retries on network failures, so creating a hold must be idempotent.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+- `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`,
+  `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU),
+  `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`;
+  enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`,
+  `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass `InventoryError`);
+  reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry
+  must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Hold**: an `ACTIVE` reservation whose `expires_at` is still in the future
+  (`now < expires_at`). Only holds reduce availability.
+- **Hold TTL**: 20 minutes by default. It can be configured through an optional
+  constructor argument, and the 3-argument constructor uses the default.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Availability
+- A1. `available(sku, wh)` = `on_hand − allocated(PLACED orders) − held units
+  of that SKU in that warehouse`, floored at 0.
+- A2. `allocated()` still counts only PLACED orders. Holds don't appear in it,
+  and the stock report gets no new column.
+- A3. `place_order` can't take held units, because it relies on `available`.
+  `place_order` stays all-or-nothing.
+- A4. Reserving, releasing, expiring and confirming never write to the ledger
+  or change on-hand.
+- A5. Holds never block `receive` or `adjust`. If a write-off pushes on-hand
+  below the held amount, `available` reports 0 and the reservations are left
+  untouched.
+
+### Reserve
+- R1. `reserve` creates an `ACTIVE` reservation with a fresh unique
+  `reservation_id`, `order_id = None`, and
+  `expires_at = clock.now() + 20 minutes` (or the configured TTL).
+- R2. Partial hold: each requested line holds `min(requested, available now)`
+  units. A line that gets 0 units is left out of `items` entirely.
+- R3. If no line can hold anything, it raises `InsufficientStock` and creates no
+  reservation.
+- R4. Validation: an unknown warehouse raises `UnknownWarehouse`, an unknown SKU
+  raises `UnknownSku`, and a non-positive or non-int quantity raises
+  `InvalidQuantity`. Empty `items` or an empty idempotency key raises an
+  `InventoryError`. A rejected request holds nothing, not even for its valid
+  lines.
+- R5. Holds held by other reservations are not available to a new reservation.
+
+### Idempotency
+- I1. Calling `reserve` again with the same key, warehouse and *requested*
+  items returns the original reservation in its current state (ACTIVE,
+  CONFIRMED, RELEASED or EXPIRED). It creates nothing new and holds nothing more.
+- I2. The same key with a different warehouse or different requested items
+  raises `IdempotencyConflict`. This also applies when the original
+  reservation is finished.
+- I3. A `reserve` call that raised an error doesn't use up its key, so a later
+  call with that key is handled as a new request.
+- I4. Keys are global across warehouses.
+
+### Expiry
+- E1. Once `clock.now() >= expires_at`, an `ACTIVE` reservation no longer
+  holds stock, and `get_reservation` reports its status as `EXPIRED`. Nobody
+  has to call a sweep for this to happen.
+- E2. Exactly at `expires_at` the reservation counts as expired.
+
+### Confirm
+- C1. Confirming a live hold creates a `PLACED` order in the reservation's
+  warehouse for exactly the held units (`items`). The reservation becomes
+  `CONFIRMED`, its `order_id` is set to the new order's ID, and the order is
+  returned.
+- C2. Confirming doesn't change `available`, because the units move from
+  "held" to "allocated". On-hand changes only when the order ships.
+- C3. Confirming an already `CONFIRMED` reservation returns the same order and
+  creates nothing, even after `expires_at` has passed.
+- C4. Confirming an expired reservation raises `ReservationExpired` and
+  creates no order.
+- C5. Confirming a `RELEASED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+### Release
+- L1. Releasing a live hold sets its status to `RELEASED` and frees the units
+  immediately. The reservation is returned.
+- L2. Releasing an already `RELEASED` reservation returns it unchanged.
+- L3. Releasing an expired reservation returns it unchanged, with status
+  `EXPIRED` and no error.
+- L4. Releasing a `CONFIRMED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+### Lookup
+- G1. `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation` raise
+  `ReservationNotFound` for an unknown ID.
+
+### Compatibility
+- K1. The existing test suite passes unchanged.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Extending or renewing a hold.
+- Confirming or releasing only part of a hold.
+- Holds that span more than one warehouse.
+- Persistence beyond the in-memory repository.
+- Listing reservations, or reporting on them.
+- Defining what happens on confirm after a write-off has pushed on-hand below
+  the held amount.
+- Any particular reservation ID format.
